# Notation
#
# Every symbol and word-phrase a claim may use is declared here. `GRAMMAR.md`
# describes the skeleton, the text around a formula; this file describes what a
# formula may contain.
#
# There are two shapes and no others. A **mixfix pattern** is a sequence of
# literal tokens and holes, written with `_` for each hole; prefix, infix,
# postfix, enclosing, application and the word predicates are all this one
# shape with the tokens in different places. **Juxtaposition** is the shape
# with no token at all, as in 2k and |CA|. A binder is a mixfix pattern with a
# hole marked as binding, which is what Agda, Coq, Isabelle and Lean all do.
#
#   pattern     the pattern, with _ for each hole. A record may carry several
#               patterns when they differ only in symbol and set.mm target;
#               the metamath list then matches the pattern list in order.
#   sort        the sort each hole takes, in order, and the sort the pattern
#               produces: `α, set of α → formula` for membership, `→ set of
#               number` for a pattern with no hole. A sort is number, point,
#               formula, group-element, `set of S`, `property of S`,
#               `function from S to S`, or a Greek letter standing for any
#               sort, shared by every hole that names it. Every notation has
#               one, and it is the only place a notation says what its holes
#               take: the parser tells readings apart by each sort's category
#               (a number, a set, a set of sets, a function, …), and a hole
#               the `binds` line says introduces a name takes a name.
#               `GRAMMAR.md` says how sorts are read off a text.
#   level       its precedence level, from the order declared below.
#   assoc       left or right. Required exactly when both edges are holes and
#               what the pattern produces fits those holes, so it can nest in itself;
#               absent otherwise, since a token at an edge or a sort mismatch
#               already settles the reading.
#   commutes    that the pattern's two holes may be exchanged without changing
#               what it denotes, so 2k and k·2 are the same number. One entry
#               per pattern, `yes` or `no`; absent where no pattern commutes.
#
#               Unlike `spells` and `negates`, the two are not the same
#               formula. The kernel writes one of them, a proof offering the
#               other has to be carried across, and the entry is what says
#               the crossing is sound. Without it a corpus that writes 2k
#               could not cite a set.mm theorem that writes k · 2, which is
#               what `divides` writes.
#   binds       for a binder, which hole introduces the variable and which
#               hole it scopes over.
#   negates     that one of the record's patterns is the negation of another,
#               written "pattern 2 is logical-not of pattern 1". The two are
#               one formula: a parser builds the same tree for both, so the
#               page may write whichever reads better and anything comparing
#               two formulas sees no difference. Four records fold a negation
#               this way, and without the line each would be a second spelling
#               nothing could match against a `not`.
#   spells      that this record's patterns are another notation written a
#               different way, as "multiplicative ·" says that juxtaposition
#               is the product written without its dot. Both build the same
#               tree, so the two spellings are one formula wherever two are
#               compared, and the page writes whichever reads better.
#   places      for a spelling that writes the other notation's holes in
#               another order, which of this record's holes fills each hole of
#               the other's, in order: `places 2 3 1` puts the condition of
#               "f(x) ≠ B for all x ∈ A" last, where "for all x ∈ A, …" has
#               it. `binds` counts this record's holes as written.
#   nests       for a spelling of a binder over several names, the name and
#               domain holes of each binder it builds, outermost first, the
#               last hole being the innermost body: `nests 1 3, 2 3` makes
#               "for all x, y ∈ ℤ, P" the tree of "for all x ∈ ℤ, for all
#               y ∈ ℤ, P".
#   bounds      for a spelling of a binder with a bound where its set stands,
#               "for all ε > 0": `bounds hole 1 by hole 2` says the name in
#               hole 1 belongs to the set the theorem's statement declares
#               for it (`ε ranges over ℝ`), and holes 1 to 2 are read as the
#               condition, which takes a hole of its own after the set.
#               `, joined to hole 3 by conjunction` puts the condition in
#               front of hole 3 instead, as "there is δ ∈ ℝ with δ > 0 and
#               …" has it.
#   joins       that the node holds two holes as one, another notation's
#               node with the two in its holes: `joins hole 3 to hole 4 by
#               conditional` makes "for all x ∈ S with A, B" the tree of
#               "for all x ∈ S, if A then B". The joined node stands where
#               the first hole did; `places` names it by that hole's number.
#   reads       how a viewer says it in words.
#   metamath    the set.mm syntax constructors the elaborator targets, said
#               the way a person checking them wants to read it.
#   target      the same thing said the way a program reads it: the term this
#               pattern builds, in the reverse Polish the kernel uses, with
#               `_1`, `_2`, `_3` standing for the pattern's holes in the order
#               they are written. So `_ + _` targets `_1 _2 caddc co` and
#               `_ divides _` targets `_1 _2 cdvds wbr`.
#
#               Three things this says that `metamath` cannot. A hole may be
#               used out of order, which is how `_ > _` targets `_2 _1 clt wbr`
#               and shares its constructor with `_ < _`. A term may carry an
#               operand the pattern has no hole for, which is how `_²` targets
#               `_1 c2 cexp co`. And the entry is `folded` for a pattern that
#               the `negates` or `spells` line turns into another notation's
#               tree, since such a pattern builds no node of its own.
#
#               A word beginning `@` stands for a part of the group the
#               theorem lets, which the page never writes: `@op` its
#               operation, `@inv` its inverse, `@subgroups` its subgroups and
#               `@lsm` the sum of two of its subsets. So `_ · _` targets
#               `_1 _2 @op co`, and the group's `let` line says which group.
#
#               A function a proof needs of its own, such as the sum of the
#               first m numbers, is a `define` in that proof and not a
#               notation here: `define S(m) := …, for m ∈ ℕ` (`GRAMMAR.md`).
#               A function the library offers, such as gcd, is a definition
#               with `sort` and `builds` lines in `corpus/stdlib`, and not a notation
#               here either: a proof file imports it by name, and gcd(a, b) is
#               read as the name gcd applied to a and b, through
#               `application-to-two`.
#
#               One entry per pattern, in pattern order. A record with no
#               `target` is one whose term is not a constructor applied to its
#               holes; an elaborator cannot build it from this file alone, and
#               `metamath` says in words what it is.
#   note        anything a reader or an implementer needs.
#
# A line beginning with # is a comment. A record begins at column 0 and its
# fields are indented.
#
# Sorts are what disambiguate. Three notations share the pattern |_|, and they
# are told apart by the sort of what is inside, which is how a reader tells
# them apart too. No rule about vertical bars appears anywhere in the grammar.
#
# They are sorts in the sense of many-sorted logic, not types: a finite set of
# syntactic categories deciding which productions apply. Nothing composes, none
# is inferred, and the label never affects meaning. Metamath has the same idea
# and calls them typecodes, of which set.mm has three; these are the readable
# layer's, one level up and eight of them.

precedence order
  # Each line names a level and the levels it binds more tightly than. Levels
  # not related here are incomparable, and a formula mixing them is rejected
  # rather than guessed at, so the text carries a parenthesis instead. This is
  # Fortress's rule. Where convention settles a pair, precedence settles it;
  # where convention does not, a reader would have to know a table, and this
  # corpus assumes no table.
  power           tighter than multiplicative
  multiplicative  tighter than unary-minus, summation
  summation       tighter than additive
  unary-minus     tighter than additive
  additive        tighter than relational
  relational      tighter than negation, predicate
  predicate       tighter than conjunction, disjunction, trailing
  negation        tighter than conjunction, disjunction, trailing
  conjunction     tighter than implication, biconditional
  disjunction     tighter than implication, biconditional
  implication     tighter than binder
  biconditional   tighter than binder
  trailing        tighter than binder
  note        `predicate` is where a relation written as words sits, and it is
              deliberately unrelated to negation: `not n is odd` does not parse,
              because a reader cannot tell it from "(not n) is odd", and
              `not (n is odd)` is written instead. A relation written as a
              symbol stays at `relational`, where `not p = 1` needs no bracket
              because nobody reads it as negating the p.

              conjunction and disjunction are deliberately unrelated, and so
              are implication and biconditional. Nobody agrees how "and" and
              "or" bind against each other, and no formula in the corpus mixes
              them. If one ever does, it writes a parenthesis.

notation number-systems
  pattern     ℕ   ℕ₀   ℤ   ℚ   ℝ   ℂ
  sort        → set of number
  reads       the natural numbers, the natural numbers with zero, the integers,
              the rationals, the reals, the complex numbers
  metamath    cn, cn0, cz, cq, cr, cc
  target      cn, cn0, cz, cq, cr, cc
  note        ℕ starts at 1, as set.mm's cn does, and ℕ₀ starts at 0. A reader
              taught that the natural numbers begin at 0 needs this pointer to
              say so. Nothing in any text assumes either convention beyond it.

notation membership
  pattern     _ ∈ _        _ ∉ _
  sort        α, set of α → formula
  level       relational
  negates     pattern 2 is logical-not of pattern 1
  reads       is in, is not in
  metamath    wcel, wnel
  target      _1 _2 wcel, folded
  note        No assoc: it yields a formula and its left hole takes a term, so
              a ∈ b ∈ c cannot parse at all rather than parsing two ways.

notation equality
  pattern     _ = _        _ ≠ _
  sort        α, α → formula
  level       relational
  negates     pattern 2 is logical-not of pattern 1
  reads       equals, is not equal to
  metamath    wceq, wne
  target      _1 _2 wceq, folded

notation order
  pattern     _ < _        _ > _        _ ≤ _        _ ≥ _
  sort        number, number → formula
  level       relational
  reads       is less than, is greater than, is at most, is at least
  metamath    clt, cle
  target      _1 _2 clt wbr, _2 _1 clt wbr, _1 _2 cle wbr, _2 _1 cle wbr

notation additive
  pattern     _ + _        _ − _
  sort        number, number → number
  level       additive
  assoc       left
  reads       plus, minus
  metamath    caddc, cmin
  target      _1 _2 caddc co, _1 _2 cmin co
  note        Left is the only defensible reading of a − b − c. No chain of
              either operator occurs in the corpus, but the parse tree is
              load-bearing for the literal-instance rule and for the expansion,
              so it is declared rather than left to each elaborator.

notation multiplicative
  pattern     _ · _        _ / _
  sort        number, number → number
  level       multiplicative
  assoc       left
  commutes    yes, no
  reads       times, divided by
  metamath    cmul, cdiv
  target      _1 _2 cmul co, _1 _2 cdiv co

notation group-operation
  pattern     _ · _
  sort        group-element, group-element → group-element
  level       multiplicative
  assoc       left
  reads       combined by the group's operation with
  metamath    the group's +g, the operation its `let` line names
  target      _1 _2 @op co
  note        Written with the dot a product is written with, and told apart
              from it by what it joins: elements of a group, which the
              group's `let` line gives their own sort. `@op` is that line's
              operation (`targets.CONTEXT`).

notation unary-minus
  pattern     −_
  sort        number → number
  level       unary-minus
  reads       minus
  metamath    cneg
  target      _1 cneg
  note        No assoc, because only the right edge is a hole: −−x can be read
              one way. Written with the same character as binary minus and told
              apart by position, not by spacing.

notation power
  pattern     _^_
  sort        number, number → number
  level       power
  assoc       right
  reads       to the power
  metamath    cexp
  target      _1 _2 cexp co
  note        Right by universal convention. No tower occurs in the corpus.
              An exponent that is not a numeral makes the whole power an atom
              to `algebra`; see METHODS.md.

notation square
  pattern     _²
  sort        number → number
  level       power
  reads       squared
  metamath    cexp with the numeral 2
  target      _1 c2 cexp co
  note        A numeral exponent, so `algebra` expands it rather than treating
              the power as an atom.

notation square-root
  pattern     √_
  sort        number → number
  level       power
  reads       the square root of
  metamath    csqrt
  target      _1 csqrt cfv

notation logical-not
  pattern     not _
  sort        formula → formula
  level       negation
  reads       not
  metamath    wn
  target      _1 wn
  note        No assoc: only the right edge is a hole.

notation conjunction
  pattern     _ and _
  sort        formula, formula → formula
  level       conjunction
  assoc       left
  reads       and
  metamath    wa
  target      _1 _2 wa
  note        Left matches set.mm's ternary conjunction, so a claim of three
              sentences maps onto the library's own lemma. See METHODS.md.

notation comma-conjunction
  pattern     _, _        _, and _
  sort        formula, formula → formula
  level       conjunction
  assoc       left
  reads       and
  metamath    wa, and nested wa for a longer list
  target      _1 _2 wa, _1 _2 wa
  note        The comma list SYNTAX.md describes, used where a run of "and"s
              reads badly, as it does inside a "there is ... with ..." phrase.
              Two patterns reach every length: the comma form builds the list
              and the comma-and form closes it, so the six clauses of
              def:stdlib/geometry/congruent need no six-hole pattern. Both are left, matching
              the plain "and" and set.mm's ternary conjunction.

              The two patterns are one notation, so the final "and" is layout:
              it builds the same tree the commas alone would, and a list
              written without it is the same formula rather than a second
              spelling of it. The comma stays available as a delimiter inside a
              pattern, as gcd(_, _) and the triangle use it, because an
              interior hole stops at the literal that follows it.

notation disjunction
  pattern     _ or _
  sort        formula, formula → formula
  level       disjunction
  assoc       left
  reads       or
  metamath    wo
  target      _1 _2 wo

notation implication
  pattern     _ → _
  sort        formula, formula → formula
  level       implication
  assoc       right
  reads       implies
  metamath    wi
  target      _1 _2 wi

notation biconditional
  pattern     _ ↔ _
  sort        formula, formula → formula
  level       biconditional
  assoc       right
  reads       if and only if
  metamath    wb
  target      _1 _2 wb
  note        ∀ and ∃ are not declared as symbols anywhere: SYNTAX.md writes
              them as the binders below, and ¬ ∧ ∨ as words.

notation conditional
  pattern     if _ then _
  sort        formula, formula → formula
  level       implication
  reads       if, then
  metamath    wi
  target      _1 _2 wi
  note        The same connective as →, written the way a definition by cases
              is read. Both spellings are in the corpus. Unlike → it needs no
              assoc, because it opens with a token: `if A then if B then C`
              has one reading where `A → B → C` has two.

notation absolute-value
  pattern     |_|
  sort        number → number
  reads       the absolute value of
  metamath    cabs
  target      _1 cabs cfv
  note        Shares its pattern with cardinality and distance and is told
              apart by the sort of its hole. Needs no assoc and no level: a
              token sits at each edge.

notation cardinality
  pattern     |_|
  sort        set of α → number
  reads       the number of elements of
  metamath    chash
  target      _1 chash cfv

notation finite
  pattern     _ is finite
  sort        set of α → formula
  level       relational
  reads       has finitely many elements
  metamath    Fin
  target      _1 cfn wcel

notation distance
  pattern     |__|
  sort        point, point → number
  reads       the distance from the first to the second
  metamath    ( abs ` ( P − Q ) )
  target      _1 _2 cmin co cabs cfv
  note        Its two holes are juxtaposed, with no token between them, so it
              is the one bar notation that is also the juxtaposition shape.
              |CA| would otherwise read as a product; the point sort is what
              rules that out, since multiplication takes numbers.

notation factorial
  pattern     _!
  sort        number → number
  level       power
  reads       factorial
  metamath    cfa
  target      _1 cfa cfv
  note        No assoc: only the left edge is a hole, so n!! reads one way.

notation mod
  pattern     _ mod _
  sort        number, number → number
  level       multiplicative
  assoc       left
  reads       the remainder on dividing by
  metamath    cmo
  target      _1 _2 cmo co
  note        The remainder, a mod b, as Euclid's algorithm writes each
              step. It binds as division does and reads left to right with
              it, so `x·y mod m` is (x·y) mod m. The word never meets the
              congruence's `(mod m)`, whose bracket opens a pattern of its
              own.

notation by-cases
  pattern     _ if _, _ otherwise
  sort        α, formula, α → α
  level       binder
  reads       the first if, and else the second
  metamath    cif
  target      _2 _1 _3 cif
  note        What a define by cases stands for: its lines, each a value and
              the condition it is taken under and the last `otherwise`, are
              this, nested from the right (`SYNTAX.md`).

notation subset
  pattern     _ ⊆ _
  sort        set of α, set of α → formula
  level       relational
  reads       is a subset of
  metamath    wss
  target      _1 _2 wss

notation imaginary-unit
  pattern     i
  sort        → number
  reads       i
  metamath    ci
  target      ci
  note        The square root of −1, mun:stdlib/numbers/i. A proof gives no
              name of its own the letter i, so that i·i = −1 on any line means
              the one thing. Geometry keeps ℂ off the page (GEOMETRY.md), and
              no geometry proof writes i.

notation empty-set
  pattern     ∅
  sort        → set of α
  reads       the empty set
  metamath    c0
  target      c0

notation singleton
  pattern     {_}
  sort        α → set of α
  reads       the set whose only element is
  metamath    csn
  target      _1 csn

notation set-operations
  pattern     _ ∪ _        _ ∖ _        _ ∩ _
  sort        set of α, set of α → set of α
  level       additive
  assoc       left
  reads       union, difference, intersection
  metamath    cun, cdif, cin
  target      _1 _2 cun, _1 _2 cdif, _1 _2 cin
  note        Difference needs the assoc for the same reason subtraction does.
              No chain of any of the three occurs in the corpus.

notation powerset
  pattern     𝒫_
  sort        set of α → set of set of α
  level       power
  reads       the power set of
  metamath    cpw
  target      _1 cpw
  note        No assoc: only the right edge is a hole.

notation supremum
  pattern     sup _
  sort        set of number → number
  level       power
  reads       the least upper bound of
  metamath    df-sup
  target      _1 cr clt csup
  note        set.mm's supremum is taken in an order and over a set of numbers,
              and the page's is always the reals under <, which is what the
              target writes. Only `axi:stdlib/calculus/completeness`'s target writes it so far:
              the least upper bound the theorem promises is this one.

notation set-builder
  pattern     {_ ∈ _ : _}
  sort        α, set of α, formula → set of α
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  reads       the set of those in the second with the third
  metamath    crab
  target      _3 _1 _2 crab
  note        Told apart from set-image by what precedes the colon: a variable
              and its domain here, an expression there. See mun:stdlib/sets/set-builder.

notation set-builder-part
  pattern     {_ ⊆ _ : _}
  sort        set of α, set of α, formula → set of set of α
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  spells      set-builder {∈:}
  wraps       hole 2 in powerset
  reads       the set of those parts of the second with the third
  metamath    crab over a power set
  target      _3 _1 _2 cpw crab
  note        "The parts X of A with …", built as {X ∈ 𝒫A : …} exactly, as
              `for-all-part` is.

notation set-image
  pattern     {_ : _ ∈ _}
  sort        β, α, set of α → set of β
  binds       hole 2 over hole 1
  reads       the set of the first as the second runs over the third
  metamath    the range of a cmpt, which is what elrnmpt speaks of
  target      _2 _3 _1 cmpt crn
  note        The binding hole is not the first one, which is why `binds` names
              the holes rather than assuming an order. See mun:stdlib/functions/set-image.

notation indexed-union
  pattern     ⋃(_ ∈ _) _
  sort        α, set of α, set of β → set of β
  level       summation
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  reads       the union, as the first runs over the second, of the third
  metamath    ciun
  target      _1 _2 _3 ciun
  note        Written as Σ is, since it gathers sets as Σ adds numbers. Where
              the set gathered is the index itself, ⋃(X ∈ D) X is set.mm's
              U. D, and `uniiun` says the two are one.

notation map
  pattern     the map sending _ ∈ _ to _
  sort        α, set of α, β → function from α to β
  level       binder
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  reads       the map sending each of the first in the second to the third
  metamath    cmpt
  target      _1 _2 _3 cmpt
  note        What set-image is the range of, which is why the two agree on
              everything but the `crn`. The corpus can say what a function
              does to a thing and what comes out of it, and until this could
              not say the function: every proof before the subsets one takes
              its function as a hypothesis rather than building one.

              Written in words rather than as `( x ∈ A ↦ B )`, which is the
              kernel's own bracketing and which the parser could not read
              anyway: an opening parenthesis is grouping and is taken before
              any pattern is tried.

notation function-type
  pattern     _ : _ → _
  sort        function from α to β, set of α, set of β → formula
  level       relational
  reads       is a function from, to
  metamath    wf
  target      _2 _3 _1 wf

notation function-on
  pattern     _ is a function on _
  sort        function from α to β, set of α → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       is a function on
  metamath    wfn
  target      _1 _2 wfn
  note        A function known by its domain alone: what a `define` with a
              parameter gives, before anything says where its values lie.
              The formula-position counterpart of `let E be a function on
              X`, as is-a-set is of `let X be a set`.

notation one-to-one
  pattern     _ : _ → _ is one-to-one
  sort        function from α to β, set of α, set of β → formula
  level       relational
  reads       is a one-to-one function from, to
  metamath    wf1
  target      _2 _3 _1 wf1
  note        A function that never sends two things to one place. The page
              keeps the arrow every function is written with and says the
              property after it, as a textbook does.

notation application
  pattern     _(_)
  sort        function from α to β, α → β
  reads       applied to
  metamath    cfv
  target      _2 _1 cfv
  note        Written identically to juxtaposition-multiplication: f(x) applies
              and k(k + 1) multiplies. The function sort of the first hole is
              what separates them, not spacing, which the corpus does not use.
              `holds-of` is the third reading of this pattern.

notation application-to-two
  pattern     _(_, _)
  sort        function from α to function from β to γ, α, β → γ
  reads       applied to, and
  metamath    co
  target      _2 _3 _1 co
  note        A function of two arguments applied to both, as gcd(a, b) is
              the library's gcd applied to a and b. Its sort takes the
              arguments one at a time, a function from the first to a
              function from the second to the value, so a function of two
              needs no sort of its own. set.mm writes an operation's value
              ( a F b ), which is the target; a library function's `builds`
              line says what an application of it stands for instead
              (`corpus/stdlib`, `definition gcd`).

notation inverse
  pattern     _⁻¹
  sort        function from α to β → function from β to α
  level       power
  reads       the inverse of
  metamath    ccnv
  target      _1 ccnv
  note        g⁻¹(y) is the thing g sends to y. set.mm's converse is defined
              for every relation, and it is a function where g is one-to-one,
              which is where the page writes it.

notation group-inverse
  pattern     _⁻¹
  sort        group-element → group-element
  level       power
  reads       the inverse in the group of
  metamath    the group's invg
  target      _1 @inv cfv
  note        g⁻¹ undoes g in the group: g·g⁻¹ = e. The pattern is the
              inverse of a function's, told apart by what it inverts.

notation subgroup
  pattern     _ is a subgroup of _
  sort        set of group-element, set of group-element → formula
  level       relational
  reads       is a subgroup of
  metamath    SubGrp of the group its `let` line names
  target      _1 @subgroups wcel
  note        The second hole names the group, which a theorem lets once;
              the kernel takes it from there (`@subgroups`).

notation coset
  pattern     __
  sort        group-element, set of group-element → set of group-element
  level       multiplicative
  assoc       right
  reads       the coset of the second by the first
  metamath    LSSum of the singleton {g} and the subgroup H
  target      _1 csn _2 @lsm co
  note        gH is {g·h : h ∈ H}. Two letters side by side are joined only
              here, and only where the first is a group element and the
              second a group's set; "and" is neither.

notation image
  pattern     _[_]
  sort        function from α to β, set of α → set of β
  reads       the image under the first of the second
  metamath    cima
  target      _1 _2 cima
  note        f[X] is everything f sends X to, {f(x) : x ∈ X}, which
              mun:stdlib/functions/image says. Square brackets and not f(X):
              f(X) is f applied to one thing, and M(C) beside f[C] in
              Schröder–Bernstein would be the two meanings side by side.

notation derivative
  pattern     _′(_)
  sort        function from number to number, number → number
  reads       the derivative of, at
  metamath    cdv over ℝ, applied with cfv
  target      _2 cr _1 cdv co cfv
  note        f′(x) is the derivative of f at x: set.mm's ( ( RR _D F ) ` x ).
              A prime on a name of a point or a number, c′ or P′, is part of
              the name; on a function applied to a term it is the derivative.

notation holds-of
  pattern     _(_)
  sort        property of α, α → formula
  reads       holds of
  metamath    substitution into a wff, which set.mm writes by naming the
              variable rather than with a constructor
  note        P(t) says the property P holds of t. It is not a function applied
              to an argument: nothing is called, and P(t) is the property with
              t written into it, which is why its target is substitution rather
              than cfv. The two readings of `_(_)` are told apart by the sort
              of the first hole, as f(x) and k(k + 1) already are.

              The braces introduce the name: in {t ∈ X : P(t)} the notation
              binds t over the condition, so a `let` line above the statement
              says what P is a property of and never mentions t, which does not
              exist until the braces open.

notation closed-interval
  pattern     [_, _]
  sort        number, number → set of number
  reads       the closed interval from, to
  metamath    cicc
  target      _1 _2 cicc co

notation open-interval
  pattern     (_, _)
  sort        number, number → set of number
  reads       the open interval from, to
  metamath    cioo
  target      _1 _2 cioo co
  note        Told apart from a bracketed term by its comma, and from a
              function applied to two arguments, f(a, b), by having no name
              before it.

notation integer-range
  pattern     {_, …, _}
  sort        number, number → set of number
  reads       the whole numbers from, to
  metamath    cfz
  target      _1 _2 cfz co
  note        The range a sum runs over, written as a set so that a line can
              say something of every index in it. A sum over {0, …, m} and a
              line "for all k ∈ {0, …, m}" name one set, which is how a
              line read at a sum's index is found. The ellipsis is what tells
              it from the singleton {_}.

notation juxtaposition
  pattern     __
  sort        number, number → number
  level       multiplicative
  assoc       left
  spells      multiplicative ·
  reads       times
  metamath    cmul
  target      folded
  note        `spells` says 2r and 2·r are one formula, which they are: both
              are cmul, and the corpus writes whichever reads better. Without
              it the √2 proof could not cite mun:stdlib/divisibility/divides, whose statement says
              n = d·k while the proof offers p = 2r.

              The one shape with no token, covering 2k and k(k + 1). It never
              joins two bare names: a·b is written with the dot, as the corpus
              writes every product of named variables. That restriction is what
              lets a run of letters be lexed without context, since `and` could
              otherwise be a·n·d and the corpus uses a, n and d as variables.
              Nineteen numeral-then-name uses and three name-then-parenthesis
              uses in the corpus, and no name-then-name use.

notation angle
  pattern     ∠___
  sort        point, point, point → number
  reads       the angle at the second from the first to the third
  metamath    the absolute value of ang applied to the two differences
  target      _1 _2 cmin co _3 _2 cmin co cang co cabs cfv
  note        Three juxtaposed holes after a token. set.mm's angle function is
              signed and it never names it, so `ang` is the one constant this
              corpus introduces; see mun:stdlib/geometry/angle. The absolute value is what
              makes ∠PQR = ∠RQP true, which the signed function does not.

notation congruent
  pattern     triangle ___ ≅ triangle ___
  sort        point, point, point, point, point, point → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       is congruent to
  metamath    the three sides and the three angles equal, as def:stdlib/geometry/congruent
              states them
  target      _1 _2 cmin co cabs cfv _4 _5 cmin co cabs cfv wceq _2 _3 cmin co
              cabs cfv _5 _6 cmin co cabs cfv wceq wa _3 _1 cmin co cabs cfv _6 _4
              cmin co cabs cfv wceq wa _1 _2 cmin co _3 _2 cmin co cang co cabs
              cfv _4 _5 cmin co _6 _5 cmin co cang co cabs cfv wceq wa _2 _3 cmin
              co _1 _3 cmin co cang co cabs cfv _5 _6 cmin co _4 _6 cmin co cang
              co cabs cfv wceq wa _3 _1 cmin co _2 _1 cmin co cang co cabs cfv _6
              _4 cmin co _5 _4 cmin co cang co cabs cfv wceq wa


# Binders. A hole marked by `binds` introduces a variable that scopes over
# another hole. SYNTAX.md writes these as words rather than as ∀ and ∃.

notation for-all
  pattern     for all _ ∈ _, _
  sort        α, set of α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  reads       for all
  metamath    wral
  target      _3 _1 _2 wral
  note        No assoc: the pattern opens with a token, so a nested binder
              announces itself and there is one reading.

notation for-all-two
  pattern     for all _, _ ∈ _, _
  sort        α, α, set of α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       holes 1 and 2 over hole 4
  reads       for all, of each
  metamath    wral twice
  target      _4 _2 _3 wral _1 _3 wral
  note        "For all x, y ∈ ℤ, …", two names over one set, as a textbook
              writes "for all a, b ∈ ℝ": the set is each name's. It is "for
              all x ∈ ℤ, for all y ∈ ℤ, …" abbreviated, and builds exactly
              that: `nests` makes one `for-all` node for each name, the
              first outermost, so either spelling meets the other.
  nests       1 3, 2 3
  spells      for-all forall∈,

notation for-all-two-part
  pattern     for all _, _ ⊆ _, _
  sort        set of α, set of α, set of α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       holes 1 and 2 over hole 4
  wraps       hole 3 in powerset
  reads       for all parts of, of each
  metamath    wral twice over a power set
  target      _4 _2 _3 cpw wral _1 _3 cpw wral
  note        "For all X, Y ⊆ A, …", built as "for all X ⊆ A, for all Y ⊆ A,
              …" is.
  nests       1 3, 2 3
  spells      for-all forall∈,

notation for-all-part
  pattern     for all _ ⊆ _, _
  sort        set of α, set of α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  wraps       hole 2 in powerset
  reads       for all parts of
  metamath    wral over a power set
  target      _3 _1 _2 cpw wral
  note        "For all X ⊆ A" is "for all X ∈ 𝒫A" in words a school reader
              has: all parts of A. It builds that formula exactly, so nothing
              downstream tells the two spellings apart. `spells` comes last
              because its value ends in a comma, which would carry it onto
              the line below.
  spells      for-all forall∈,

notation for-all-with
  pattern     for all _ ∈ _ with _, _
  sort        α, set of α, formula, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       hole 1 over holes 3 and 4
  joins       hole 3 to hole 4 by conditional
  spells      for-all forall∈,
  reads       for all, with
  metamath    wral, with wi inside it
  target      _3 _4 wi _1 _2 wral
  note        The restricted universal, "for all ε ∈ ℝ with ε > 0, …". It is
              "for all ε ∈ ℝ, if ε > 0 then …" and builds exactly that tree:
              `joins` holds the condition and the body as the `if … then`
              node, and `spells` makes the whole a `for-all` node, so either
              spelling meets the other.

              "with" means a conjunction after "there is" and an implication
              here, which is ordinary mathematical English and is why the two
              join by different notations. The side condition is delimited by
              the comma, so a condition that is itself a comma list would need
              brackets; none is written and none reads well.

notation for-all-after
  pattern     _ for all _ ∈ _
  sort        formula, α, set of α → formula
  level       trailing
  assoc       left
  binds       hole 2 over hole 1
  reads       for all, written after what it says
  metamath    wral
  target      _1 _2 _3 wral
  note        "f(x) ≠ B for all x ∈ A", the universal written after its
              condition, as a textbook writes one inside "such that". The
              same formula as "for all x ∈ A, f(x) ≠ B". The condition is a
              single relation, predicate or negation: `trailing` is unrelated
              to "and", "or", "if … then" and "if and only if", so "P and Q
              for all x ∈ A" is refused rather than read one way, and the page
              writes "(P and Q) for all x ∈ A" or the prefix form. Two in a
              row, "P for all x ∈ A for all y ∈ B", are both over P.

              It builds `for-all`'s node, its holes put in that notation's
              order by `places`, so a claim written either way meets an item
              written the other.
  places      2 3 1
  spells      for-all forall∈,

notation for-all-after-part
  pattern     _ for all _ ⊆ _
  sort        formula, set of α, set of α → formula
  level       trailing
  assoc       left
  binds       hole 2 over hole 1
  wraps       hole 3 in powerset
  reads       for all parts of, written after what it says
  metamath    wral over a power set
  target      _1 _2 _3 cpw wral
  note        "… for all X ⊆ A", built as `for-all-part` builds "for all X ⊆
              A, …".
  places      2 3 1
  spells      for-all forall∈,

notation for-all-after-bounded
  pattern     _ for all _ > _        _ for all _ ≥ _        _ for all _ < _        _ for all _ ≤ _        _ for all _ ≠ _
  sort        formula, α, α → formula
  level       trailing
  assoc       left
  binds       hole 2 over hole 1
  bounds      hole 2 by hole 3, joined to hole 1 by conditional
  spells      for-all forall∈,
  reads       for all, written after what it says, with a bound in place of the set
  metamath    wral, with wi inside it
  target      folded, folded, folded, folded, folded
  note        "… < ε for all n ≥ N", as a textbook writes it inside "such
              that", where the theorem says what n ranges over. It builds
              what "for all n ∈ ℕ, if n ≥ N then …" builds, so it meets
              "for all n ≥ N, …" and "for all n ∈ ℕ with n ≥ N, …". What it
              says is one relation, as for any trailing universal.

notation for-all-after-with
  pattern     _ for all _ ∈ _ with _
  sort        formula, α, set of α, formula → formula
  level       trailing
  assoc       left
  binds       hole 2 over holes 1 and 4
  joins       hole 4 to hole 1 by conditional
  places      2 3 4
  spells      for-all forall∈,
  reads       for all, with, written after what it says
  metamath    wral, with wi inside it
  target      _4 _1 wi _2 _3 wral
  note        "|f(x) − f(c)| < ε for all x ∈ D with |x − c| < δ", as a
              textbook writes continuity. It builds what "for all x ∈ D, if
              |x − c| < δ then |f(x) − f(c)| < ε" builds: `joins` holds the
              condition and what precedes as the `if … then` node, where the
              condition stood, and `places` puts the name, the set and that
              node in `for-all`'s order. What it says and the condition are
              each one relation: the condition stands at the right edge,
              where a looser connective would swallow what follows.

notation there-is
  pattern     there is _ ∈ _ with _        there is no _ ∈ _ with _
  sort        α, set of α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  negates     pattern 2 is logical-not of pattern 1
  reads       there is, there is no
  metamath    wrex, and wrex under wn
  target      _3 _1 _2 wrex, folded

notation there-is-part
  pattern     there is _ ⊆ _ with _
  sort        set of α, set of α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  spells      there-is thereis∈with
  wraps       hole 2 in powerset
  reads       there is a part of, with
  metamath    wrex over a power set
  target      _3 _1 _2 cpw wrex
  note        "There is C ⊆ A with …" is "there is C ∈ 𝒫A with …", built
              as exactly that formula, as `for-all-part` is.

notation there-exists
  pattern     there exists _ ∈ _ such that _
  sort        α, set of α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  reads       there exists, such that
  metamath    wrex
  target      folded
  note        "There exists x ∈ S such that …" is "there is x ∈ S with …" in
              the words a textbook states a theorem in, and builds the same
              formula, so the page writes whichever reads better.
  spells      there-is thereis∈with

notation there-exists-part
  pattern     there exists _ ⊆ _ such that _
  sort        set of α, set of α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  spells      there-is thereis∈with
  wraps       hole 2 in powerset
  reads       there exists a part of, such that
  metamath    wrex over a power set
  target      _3 _1 _2 cpw wrex
  note        "There exists C ⊆ A such that …", built as there-is-part builds
              "there is C ⊆ A with …".

notation for-all-bounded
  pattern     for all _ > _, _        for all _ ≥ _, _        for all _ < _, _        for all _ ≤ _, _        for all _ ≠ _, _
  sort        α, α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  bounds      hole 1 by hole 2, joined to hole 3 by conditional
  spells      for-all forall∈,
  reads       for all, with a bound in place of the set
  metamath    wral, with wi inside it
  target      folded, folded, folded, folded, folded
  note        "For all ε > 0, …", as a textbook writes it, where the theorem
              says what ε ranges over: `ε ranges over ℝ` in its statement.
              It builds exactly what "for all ε ∈ ℝ, if ε > 0 then …" builds,
              and so what "for all ε ∈ ℝ with ε > 0, …" does, the set being
              the declared one and the condition "ε > 0" read as it is
              written, so any of the three meets the others. A name
              no range line covers has no reading here, and the page writes
              the set. `∈` is no bound, or "for all x ∈ S, …" would read two
              ways.

notation there-is-bounded
  pattern     there is _ > _ with _        there is _ ≥ _ with _        there is _ < _ with _        there is _ ≤ _ with _        there is _ ≠ _ with _
  sort        α, α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  bounds      hole 1 by hole 2, joined to hole 3 by conjunction
  spells      there-is thereis∈with
  reads       there is, with a bound in place of the set
  metamath    wrex
  target      folded, folded, folded, folded, folded
  note        "There is δ > 0 with …", where the theorem says what δ ranges
              over. It builds what "there is δ ∈ ℝ with δ > 0 and …" builds:
              the condition goes in front of what follows "with", at the far
              left of a run of conjunctions, as the long form writes it.

notation there-exists-bounded
  pattern     there exists _ > _ such that _        there exists _ ≥ _ such that _        there exists _ < _ such that _        there exists _ ≤ _ such that _        there exists _ ≠ _ such that _
  sort        α, α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  bounds      hole 1 by hole 2, joined to hole 3 by conjunction
  spells      there-is thereis∈with
  reads       there exists, with a bound in place of the set, such that
  metamath    wrex
  target      folded, folded, folded, folded, folded
  note        "There exists δ > 0 such that …", the words a textbook states a
              theorem in, built as "there is δ > 0 with …" builds it.

notation there-is-some
  pattern     there is _ ∈ _
  sort        α, set of α → formula
  level       binder
  binds       hole 1 over nothing
  reads       there is
  metamath    wrex over a true body; set.mm states it as ≠ ∅
  target      wtru _1 _2 wrex
  note        Nonemptiness, with a name for the element and nothing said about
              it. Three uses: the statement of mun:stdlib/counting/card-nonempty and a
              hypothesis each of axi:stdlib/numbers/well-ordering and axi:stdlib/calculus/completeness.

              The name binds over nothing, which no other binder does. Writing
              X ≠ ∅ instead would need no record and is what set.mm states, but
              the subsets proof reads "obtain a: mun:stdlib/counting/card-nonempty", and an
              obtain takes its name from an existence claim that names one. So
              the conclusion has to name an element, and the two hypotheses are
              written the same way rather than two ways.

notation there-are
  pattern     there are _ ∈ _ and _ ∈ _ with _
  sort        α, set of α, β, set of β, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       holes 1 and 3 over hole 5
  reads       there are, and, with
  metamath    wrex nested twice, one for each variable
  target      _5 _3 _4 wrex _1 _2 wrex
  note        The two-variable form, for two names over two sets, as
              mun:stdlib/numbers/rational has p ∈ ℤ and q ∈ ℕ. Writing it as
              two nested `there is` phrases reads far worse. Two names over
              one set are written "there are p, q ∈ ℤ with …", which builds
              this node.

notation there-are-two
  pattern     there are _, _ ∈ _ with _
  sort        α, α, set of α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       holes 1 and 2 over hole 4
  reads       there are, of each, with
  metamath    wrex nested twice, one for each variable
  target      _4 _2 _3 wrex _1 _3 wrex
  note        "There are x, y ∈ ℤ with …", two names over one set: the set is
              each name's. It is "there are x ∈ ℤ and y ∈ ℤ with …"
              abbreviated, and builds exactly that node, the set filling both
              of its set holes by `places`, so either spelling meets the
              other.
  places      1 3 2 3 4
  spells      there-are thereare∈and∈with

notation there-exist-two
  pattern     there exist _, _ ∈ _ such that _
  sort        α, α, set of α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       holes 1 and 2 over hole 4
  reads       there exist, of each, such that
  metamath    wrex nested twice, one for each variable
  target      _4 _2 _3 wrex _1 _3 wrex
  note        "There exist x, y ∈ ℤ such that …", the words a textbook states
              a theorem in, as "there exists … such that" is for one name.
              It builds `there-are`'s node as "there are x, y ∈ ℤ with …"
              does.
  places      1 3 2 3 4
  spells      there-are thereare∈and∈with

notation for-all-sets
  pattern     for all sets _, _
  sort        set of α, formula → formula
  level       binder
  binds       hole 1 over hole 2
  reads       for all sets
  metamath    wal, with no restricting domain
  target      _2 _1 wal
  note        The domain-free binder, quantifying over an arbitrary set rather
              than over the elements of a named one. It is the formula-position
              counterpart of the hypothesis line `let X be a set`, exactly as
              for-all is of `let n ∈ ℕ`. Three uses, all in the subsets proof,
              whose induction is over a statement universally quantified across
              sets. A sibling for points or functions would be a new record here
              and nothing else; the corpus wants neither yet.


# Word predicates. Each is introduced by a definition, which is what makes the
# closed set of shapes matter: adding one is a record here and a definition
# there, never a change to the parser.

notation divides
  pattern     _ divides _
  sort        number, number → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       divides
  metamath    wbr with cdvds
  target      _1 _2 cdvds wbr
  note        Seventeen uses, the commonest word predicate. See mun:stdlib/divisibility/divides.

notation congruent-mod
  pattern     _ ≡ _ (mod _)
  sort        number, number, number → formula
  level       relational
  reads       is congruent to, modulo
  metamath    wbr with cdvds, of a difference
  target      _3 _1 _2 cmin co cdvds wbr
  note        a ≡ b (mod n) says n divides a − b, which is Hammack's definition
              and what the target writes, so a congruence and the divisibility
              it abbreviates are one formula and every lemma about the one is
              about the other.

notation sum
  pattern     Σ(_ = _ to _) _
  sort        number, number, number, number → number
  level       summation
  binds       hole 1 over hole 4
  reads       the sum, from, to, of
  metamath    csu over ( M ... N )
  target      _2 _3 cfz co _4 _1 csu
  note        The sum of the fourth hole for each whole number from the second
              to the third. It binds tighter than + and −, as a reader expects:
              Σ(k = 0 to n) d(k)·10^k − Σ(k = 0 to n) d(k) is one sum less
              another, and a sum of a sum or a difference writes its brackets.

notation series
  pattern     Σ(_ = _ to ∞) _
  sort        number, number, number → number
  level       summation
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  reads       the sum, from, without end, of
  metamath    csu over ( ZZ>= ` M )
  target      _2 cuz cfv _3 _1 csu
  note        The sum of the third hole for every whole number from the second
              on: the value of the series, which mun:stdlib/calculus/series-sum
              says is the limit of its partial sums. set.mm sums over the
              upper integers from M, and ℕ is those from 1.

notation sum-over
  pattern     Σ(_ ∈ _) _
  sort        number, set of number, number → number
  level       summation
  binds       hole 1 over hole 3
  reads       the sum, as the first runs over the second, of the third
  metamath    csu over a finite set
  target      _2 _3 _1 csu
  note        The sum of the third hole for each member of the second, which is
              finite. Σ(k = a to b) is this sum over {a, …, b}, and is kept
              because a reader writes a range that way.

notation sum-over-condition
  pattern     Σ(_ ∈ _ : _) _
  sort        number, set of number, formula, number → number
  level       summation
  binds       hole 1 over holes 3 and 4
  reads       the sum, as the first runs over those in the second with the third, of the fourth
  metamath    csu over crab, one letter binding both
  target      _3 _1 _2 crab _4 _1 csu
  note        The sum over the members of a set that meet a condition, with one
              letter for both, as a reader writes it: σ(n) is
              Σ(d ∈ ℕ : d divides n) d, the sum of the divisors of n. It is
              the sum over a set built by `{_ ∈ _ : _}`, written without
              naming the set.

notation tends-to
  pattern     _ → _ as _ → ∞
  sort        number, number, number → formula
  level       relational
  binds       hole 3 over hole 1
  reads       tends to, as, grows without end
  metamath    ~~> of the sequence ( n e. NN |-> a )
  target      _3 cn _1 cmpt _2 cli wbr
  note        A sequence tending to a limit, written as a textbook writes it:
              the first hole is its n-th term, and the third names n. The
              sequence is indexed by ℕ. set.mm's `~~>` is a relation between
              the sequence and the limit, so this names that relation and
              introduces nothing; def:stdlib/calculus/tends-to says what it
              means in ε and N.

notation parity
  pattern     _ is even        _ is odd        _ is not odd
  sort        number → formula
  level       predicate
  negates     pattern 3 is logical-not of pattern 2
  reads       is even, is odd, is not odd
  metamath    2 ∥ n and its negation
  target      c2 _1 cdvds wbr, c2 _1 cdvds wbr wn, folded
  note        "is not odd" keeps the word inside the phrase, which is how the
              corpus reads best, and the `negates` line is what makes it the
              same formula as "not (n is odd)". Without it the √2 proof could
              not cite mun:stdlib/reasoning/disjunctive-syllogism, which assumes "not Q".

notation prime
  pattern     _ is prime
  sort        number → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       is prime
  metamath    wcel with cprime
  target      _1 cprime wcel

notation perfect
  pattern     _ is perfect
  sort        number → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       is perfect
  metamath    σ(n) = 2n, which set.mm writes and never names
  target      c1 _1 csgm co c2 _1 cmul co wceq
  note        A number is perfect when the sum of its divisors is twice it, which
              is what the target writes, so the word and the equation are one
              formula; def:stdlib/divisibility/perfect says so for the reader.

notation irrational
  pattern     _ is irrational
  sort        number → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       is irrational
  metamath    wcel with ℝ ∖ ℚ
  target      _1 cr cq cdif wcel

notation is-a-point
  pattern     _ is a point
  sort        point → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       is a point
  metamath    wcel with ℂ, the plane
  target      _1 cc wcel
  note        The counterpart of the `let A be a point` introduction, as
              is-a-set is of `let X be a set`. What a point is made of is the
              encoding and the reader does not meet it: the page says a point
              and never says ℂ. GEOMETRY.md takes that decision.

notation is-a-set
  pattern     _ is a set
  sort        set of α → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       is a set
  metamath    wcel with _V, as difexg states it
  target      _1 cvv wcel
  note        Sethood, which ZF distinguishes from being a proper class and
              set.mm writes as membership of _V. One use, mun:stdlib/sets/difference-set;
              a proof does not write that a set built from sets is a set
              (`READERS.md`). It is the formula-position counterpart of the
              `let X be a set` introduction, as for-all-sets is of the same
              line in a binder.

notation bound
  pattern     _ is an upper bound of _        _ is a least upper bound of _
  sort        number, set of number → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       is an upper bound of, is a least upper bound of
  metamath    see mun:stdlib/calculus/upper-bound and mun:stdlib/calculus/least-upper-bound
  target      vs cv _1 cle wbr vs _2 wral,
              vs cv _1 cle wbr vs _2 wral vs cv vt cv cle wbr vs _2 wral
              _1 vt cv cle wbr wi vt cr wral wa
  note        set.mm has no upper bound of its own: `df-sup` builds the idea
              inside a class abstraction and nothing names it. So each entry
              is the quantified formula `mun:stdlib/calculus/upper-bound` and
              `mun:stdlib/calculus/least-upper-bound` state, which is what those two say the
              notation means. `s` ranges over the set and `t` over the reals
              the second pattern compares against; neither is a hole, and
              reserving them is what stops a proof that fixes an `s` of its
              own from being handed the same variable.

notation continuous
  pattern     _ is continuous on _
  sort        function from number to number, set of number → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       is continuous on
  metamath    ccncf
  target      _1 _2 cr ccncf co wcel
  note        set.mm has no predicate for continuity at all: `-cn->` takes a
              domain and a codomain and gives the set of continuous functions
              between them, so continuity is membership of that set. The
              codomain is the operand no hole fills. The readable pattern
              names only the domain because `def:stdlib/calculus/continuous-on` takes
              `f : D → ℝ` as its hypothesis, so the codomain is ℝ wherever
              this pattern may be written.

notation differentiable
  pattern     _ is differentiable on _
  sort        function from number to number, set of number → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       is differentiable on
  metamath    the domain of the derivative, cdm of cdv over ℝ
  target      _2 cr _1 cdv co cdm wss
  note        set.mm has no predicate for this either. The target says the
              derivative of f is defined at every point of the set, as a
              textbook means it: x² is differentiable on (0, 1) though it is
              differentiable everywhere. mvth and rolle ask the derivative be
              defined on exactly the open interval, which for f on [a, b]
              follows, since no derivative is taken at an end (gdvdmicc).

notation collinear
  pattern     _, _, _ are collinear
  sort        point, point, point → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       are collinear
  metamath    the ratio of the two differences is real
  target      _3 _1 cmin co _2 _1 cmin co cdiv co cr wcel
  note        Three points on one line, which mun:stdlib/geometry/triangle needs to say that a
              triangle's are not. Its definition is open, as mun:stdlib/geometry/angle is while
              the ∠ notation stands declared: incidence is a primitive of the
              geometry, and what it means in set.mm's plane is the same
              question mun:stdlib/geometry/point leaves open.

notation forms-a-triangle
  pattern     _, _, _ form a triangle
  sort        point, point, point → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       form a triangle
  metamath    the three vertices distinct and not on one line
  target      _1 _2 wceq wn _2 _3 wceq wn wa _1 _3 wceq wn wa
              _3 _1 cmin co _2 _1 cmin co cdiv co cr wcel wn wa

notation bijection
  pattern     there is a bijection from _ to _
  sort        set of α, set of β → formula
  level       predicate
  reads       there is a bijection from, to
  metamath    wbr with cen
  target      _1 _2 cen wbr
  note        Not a binder despite the "there is": it names no variable, and
              the subsets pilot records that the language has no way to name a
              function built on the spot.
